  "query_preview": "..\nToday: The shortwave that brought showers to the region \nyesterday has moved out of the area. However, lingering \ninstability with CAPE values 200-500 J/kg will bring a chance at\ncontinued afternoon and evening thunderstorms. The east slopes \nof the Cascades have the largest chance (20 to 30 percent) at \nthunderstorms. Main impacts with these thunderstorms will be \nisolated heavy downpours, fire starts due to lightning, and \noutflow wind gusts up to 30 mph due to 500-600 J/kg of DCAPE. \nIsolated heavy downpours especially in steep terrain and near \nburn scars bring the chance of debris flows. As such, a Red Flag\nWarning is valid for the east Cascades through 10pm this \nevening. \n\nFriday through Wednesday: The trough over the area will \nretrograde westward off the coast due as the offshore low moves \nnorthward. This will stay in roughly the same place through \nTuesday of next week, and will result in mostly southwesterly \nflow through these days. Instability continues to ramp up each \nafternoon and evening due to warming temperatures and continued \nhigher than normal PWATs (120-150 percent of normal). Main \nthings to keep an eye on will be any small shortwaves that may \nmove through over the weekend. Right now, it looks as though the\nSE end of Spokane County may experience a quick thunderstorm or\ntwo on Saturday as a shortwave moves in. On Monday through \nTuesday, a trough will drop down over the state of Washington. \nWhile much of the convection prior to this will be mostly \nconfined to the mountains and ID Panhandle, thunderstorm chances\nbecome more widespread on these days. Due to impacts from these\nupcoming thunderstorms being very similar to those seen \nyesterday and today, we'll continue to monitor each day for fire\nweather concerns associated with lightning and gusty winds. \n\nWednesday through Friday: Cluster analysis and long-term models\nremain in disagreement over whether we will continue seeing a \ncooler and wetter pattern or transition to a warmer and drier \npattern. Roughly half of the clusters show much of the PNW in a \ndrier and warmer pattern to end next week, while the other half \nshows us continuing to see lower heights. This will depend on \nwhether the low off the west coast strengthens or dissipates. \nOverall: expect unsettled weather conditions to continue with \nchances of afternoon and evening thunderstorms in mountain \nareas. Temperatures will remain very close to or just below \nnormal through the next week with only Minor HeatRisk expected. \n/AS",
